"""
check-leaks — extract secrets from a dump (git objects, repo checkout, JS
bundles…) and check which ones are live, ONLY through read-only identity calls
(whoami). Only the HTTP status is looked at: no content read, nothing written,
no message sent.

Usage:
  check-leaks <dir> [options]

  --offline        no network at all (JWT decoding, key fingerprints, DB/SAS classification)
  --no-generic     disable the generic name="value" extractor
  --exclude=a,b    skip these directory names (e.g. upstream forks)
  --hosts=h1,h2:p  compare found private keys with the TLS key these hosts serve now
  --domains=a.com  target domains: collect hostnames under them from the dump and
                   check their live TLS key; flag crt.sh certificates for them
  --no-crtsh       skip the crt.sh lookup of private keys
  --pk-all         also triage keys found in TEST / VENDOR files

Coverage: cloud (AWS, GCP service accounts, Azure connection strings / SAS),
SaaS tokens, webhooks, DB URIs (DNS + TCP only, NEVER authenticates), private
keys (SPKI fingerprint, dedup, crt.sh + live TLS comparison), JWT, generic.

Outputs (current directory):
  leaks_checked.json      one entry per test, with source file and context
  generic_candidates.tsv  high-entropy values assigned to secret-like names
"""
